        "name": "Colin Wright",
        "text": "🚨ALERT: The editors of the journal Men and Masculinities will no longer allow authors to refer to individuals as being \"male.\"\n\nIf an author uses the term, they should \"specify which dimension(s) of sex are at issue, since sex itself refers to several only loosely correlated dimensions rather than a single underlying fact.\"\n\nFor example, trans-identifying male swimmer Lia Thomas must be referred to as a woman, and his maleness, if mentioned at all, must be attached to specific traits, like genitals, hormones, and chromosomes.\n\nYou cannot call Lia Thomas a \"male\" in this journal, but a woman with \"male genitals\" and \"male sex chromosomes.\"\n\nThe first author of this editorial—the Managing Editor of Men and Masculinities, Tristen Kade—is a woman who identifies as a man.\n\nThe model of biological sex this editorial is pushing is what I refer to in my scholarly article \"Why There Are Exactly Two Sexes\" as the \"multi-level\" sex model. This model is not merely wrong—it is self-refuting. \n\nFrom my article:\n\n\"Traits are labeled 'male-typical' or 'female-typical' only because they correlate with organisms already identified as male or female—an identification that, in anisogamous species, is made ultimately by reference to gametes.\"\n\nRead my article at the link below.\n🔗https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10508-025-03348-3",
